# Spooky Author Identification — swarm solution (aldebaran_xi)

3-class author classification (EAP/HPL/MWS) on sentence text. Metric: **multi-class log loss**.
Final artifact: `submission.csv` (columns `id,EAP,HPL,MWS`, 1958 rows).

## Summary of approach
Two-level ensemble:
1. **Base models** (all on the *same* validation partition):
   - Classical sparse text models: TF-IDF word/char n-grams + LogisticRegression / LinearSVC / SGD / MultinomialNB / ComplementNB / NB-SVM, plus LightGBM on SVD+stylometry.
   - Transformer fine-tunes: `roberta-base`, `roberta-large`, `microsoft/deberta-v3-base` (2 seeds), `microsoft/deberta-v3-large`, `google/electra-base-discriminator`, `xlm-roberta-base`, and a frozen MPNet embedding model.
2. **Stacker**: multinomial `LogisticRegression(C=0.1)` on `concat(log p)` of every base model with solo OOF log loss < 0.65. Final test predictions = average of 10 stackers each fit on a random 80% of the OOF matrix (variance reduction).

## Validation (honest, train-only)
`StratifiedKFold(n_splits=5, shuffle=True, random_state=42)` over `data/train.csv` in original row order.
Every agent used exactly this split (verified: per-fold log loss of each transformer reproduces the fold scores in its `meta_*.json`). Class order **EAP,HPL,MWS**.
The stacker score is estimated with *nested* CV (3× repeated 5-fold on the OOF matrix, seeds 100/101/102), never on the hidden test.
`train.csv` (17621) + `test.csv` (1958) = 19579 = the original public train, so the hidden test is a random 10% slice and OOF/nested-CV are expected to track it closely.
Early stopping for every transformer used only its fold's training/validation split (no test data). No test labels were read at any point.

**Nested OOF log loss of the final stack: see `stack_meta.json` (`nested_oof_logloss`).**

## Files
- `submission.csv` — final predictions.
- `solution.py` — reproduces `submission.csv` from the base-model prediction matrices (`workspaces/*/preds/{oof,test}_*.npy`): discovery, filtering, nested-CV estimate, bagged stacker, writes submission.
- `train_transformer.py` — transformer fine-tuning code (5-fold, fold-averaged test preds, saves `oof_*.npy`/`test_*.npy`).
- `classical_models.py` — TF-IDF + linear/NB baseline code (word 1-2/1-3 + char_wb n-grams).
- `stack_meta.json` — nested score, stacker hyper-parameters and the exact list of member models with solo OOF.

## External checkpoints (source = Hugging Face Hub, via `transformers`)
| model ID | purpose | license |
|---|---|---|
| `FacebookAI/roberta-base` | base fine-tune (diversity) | MIT |
| `FacebookAI/roberta-large` | base fine-tune (strong member) | MIT |
| `microsoft/deberta-v3-base` | base fine-tune, seeds 42 & 123 | MIT |
| `microsoft/deberta-v3-large` | base fine-tune (strongest single model) | MIT |
| `google/electra-base-discriminator` | base fine-tune (diversity) | Apache-2.0 |
| `xlm-roberta-base` | base fine-tune (different tokenizer, diversity) | MIT |
| `sentence-transformers/all-mpnet-base-v2` | frozen sentence embeddings -> linear classifier (weak diverse member) | Apache-2.0 |

All are general-purpose pretrained language models; none was trained on this competition's labels and none is a competition solution. Revision: default `main` at run time (2026-10-30). No external APIs, no web search, no pretrained competition-specific checkpoints.

## Notes
- Post-hoc temperature/prior calibration was tested on OOF and gave no gain (T=1 optimal; predicted class prior already 0.402/0.288/0.310).
- Weak members are intentionally kept: the stack uses them for decorrelation (simple averaging is much worse).
